## Written answers (Part 4)

**Why is prior conversation context resent with every turn?**
The model is stateless. Each call sees only the messages passed in that call;
it retains nothing between calls. The apparent memory is maintained entirely by
the client, which appends each user and assistant message to a list and resends
the whole list every turn.

**How is a system prompt different from a user message?**
The system prompt defines role and persistent constraints, sits first in the
message list, and is resent every turn, so it applies to the whole session.
AGENT.md is the example here: it constrains how the model answers. A user
message is a single-turn request specifying what to answer.

**Why do input tokens grow over a conversation?**
Because the full history is resent each turn and the history only grows.
Measured here: 112, 212, 308, 431, 549 input tokens across five turns. The
increment itself grows, so cumulative input cost is quadratic while the history
length is linear. At turn 5 cumulative input was 1612 against a history length
of 647.

**What eventually limits that growth?**
The context window, the maximum number of tokens the model can process in one
call. Beyond it the request fails or the oldest messages are silently dropped.
Mitigations include sliding-window truncation, summarising older turns, and
retrieving from an external memory store on demand.
